const READING_DEEP_DIVE={1:{
1:[
['Why the question comes first','The handbook presents data analysis as a process rather than a collection of isolated calculations. Begin by identifying the purpose of the work: what decision, problem or improvement is the analysis meant to support? From that purpose, form specific questions. A question gives the analysis direction. Without it, an analyst can spend time producing attractive charts that answer nothing important.'],
['Data is evidence, not an answer','Data can be a number, word, picture, map, record or another observation. A raw value is evidence, but it does not automatically explain itself. To make the evidence useful, establish what it represents, where it came from, when it was collected, and how it relates to the purpose of the analysis.'],
['Data versus information','A value such as 1,099 is data. If we learn that 1,099 represents enrolled students during a defined period, we have added context that makes the value informative. The distinction matters because analysts often work with large collections of raw observations and must turn them into information that supports understanding and decisions.'],
['Context protects against false comparisons','Imagine two tables both contain the value 80. One might mean 80 customers; another might mean 80 percent. Even two customer counts may not be comparable if one covers a week and another covers a year. Before comparing values, identify the unit, population, category definitions, date or period, and source.'],
['Patterns are clues, not explanations','Sorting, grouping and visualising data can expose a difference or pattern. That is valuable because it tells you where to look next. But a visible pattern does not prove why it occurred. If sales rise sharply in one month, an analyst should investigate timing, events, data quality and other evidence before claiming a cause.'],
['Observation, interpretation and recommendation','Keep three levels separate. An observation states what the evidence shows. Interpretation considers what that observation may mean in relation to the question. A recommendation proposes an action based on the findings. Mixing these levels can make an opinion look like a measured result.'],
['A defensible analyst habit','Write the question before writing the conclusion. Keep the source, definitions, period and important assumptions visible. Explain the finding so that another person can follow the reasoning without having to recreate the analysis from scratch.'],
['Study application','Imagine a shop reports 240 orders in April and 310 in May. Do not immediately say “sales improved.” First ask whether orders are the intended measure, whether the periods are comparable, whether the counts represent unique customers or transactions, and what other evidence is needed. The disciplined conclusion might begin with the observed change and then identify what remains unknown.']
],
2:[
['From raw values to useful evidence','The handbook explains that data must be gathered and sorted before it can be interpreted. A spreadsheet is therefore not automatically an analysis. The analyst has to connect observations to definitions, questions and context. The same dataset can support different analyses depending on the purpose.'],
['Qualitative and quantitative evidence','Quantitative data is expressed numerically. Qualitative data is commonly represented through words or narrative descriptions. Neither is automatically superior. The useful choice depends on the question, and some investigations benefit from using both types.'],
['Quantitative data in practice','Counts, measurements and numerical values can be quantitative evidence. Examples include number of customers, temperature, revenue and age. But always ask what the number represents. A number can also be a category label rather than a measurement.'],
['Qualitative data in practice','Interviews, open-ended questionnaire responses, notes, minutes and observations can provide narrative evidence. Such evidence can describe experiences, reasons, perceptions or context that a numerical count alone may not reveal.'],
['The collection method matters','How evidence was produced affects how it should be interpreted. A closed survey question may produce a count or category code; an open-ended question may produce narrative responses; an interview may produce a transcript. Understanding the collection method helps prevent inappropriate analysis.'],
['Numbers are not automatically measurements','Suppose a survey records 1 for Yes and 2 for No. Those numbers are labels for categories. Calculating an average of 1 and 2 would not create a meaningful average opinion. Classification must come before calculation.'],
['Mixed evidence can strengthen an answer','A business might have weekly sales figures and customer comments. The numerical data can show amounts and changes, while the comments can reveal experiences or recurring issues. The two types should be analysed according to their own meanings and then brought together around the same question.'],
['A difficult classification case','A customer survey contains a 1–5 satisfaction rating and an optional written explanation. The rating is numerical evidence, while the explanation is qualitative evidence. Treating the explanation as if it were a numerical score would discard its meaning; treating the rating as narrative would also lose information.'],
['Study application','Before choosing a method, take ten observations from a real or simulated dataset. For each one, identify what it represents, how it was collected, whether it is quantitative, qualitative or part of a mixed source, and what question it could reasonably help answer.']
],
3:[
['Classification before calculation','The handbook’s distinction between quantitative and qualitative evidence is practical: identify what the evidence represents before deciding how to work with it. This prevents an analyst from applying numerical operations to values that are only labels or from forcing narrative evidence into an unsuitable numerical format.'],
['A number can be a label','A field containing 1, 2 and 3 may look numerical. But if those values represent Basic, Standard and Premium membership categories, the numbers are codes. Their mathematical appearance does not make them measurements. Ask what the field means before calculating.'],
['A workflow for evidence','A defensible beginner workflow is: define the question, inspect the source, identify the collection method, classify the evidence, check definitions and then choose an analysis procedure. This sequence keeps method selection connected to purpose rather than habit.'],
['Surveys can be mixed','One survey may contain age, monthly spending, a yes/no response and an open-ended comment. These fields do not all carry the same kind of evidence. The analyst should preserve their distinctions instead of converting everything into a single artificial scale.'],
['Interviews and narrative evidence','An interview transcript can contain detailed descriptions and repeated ideas. The useful work is not simply counting words; it may involve identifying themes, comparing responses and connecting those themes to the question. The exact method should follow the purpose and the nature of the evidence.'],
['Quality before sophistication','A sophisticated method cannot rescue evidence that is poorly defined or irrelevant to the question. Before choosing an advanced technique, establish what the observations mean, whether the source is appropriate, and whether the data actually addresses the problem.'],
['Preserve the evidence trail','Record where the evidence came from, what period it covers, what categories mean and what transformations were made. A second analyst should be able to understand the important choices that led from source data to finding.'],
['Case study','A company stores department codes as 1=Sales, 2=Support and 3=Operations. It also stores monthly revenue and customer comments. Revenue can be analysed numerically; department codes identify categories; comments provide qualitative evidence. The correct workflow keeps those roles distinct while allowing them to answer a common business question.'],
['Study application','Classify a small dataset before calculating anything. For each field, write: meaning, source, collection method, evidence type, valid operations and one question it could help answer. If you cannot explain the field, stop and investigate before analysing it.']
],
4:[
['Visualising data','The handbook describes visualisation as a useful way to create a visual picture of data and begin examining it. A chart can make differences, clusters or unusual values easier to notice than a long list. But visualisation is not a substitute for analysis.'],
['Exploratory analysis','Exploratory analysis is useful when there is a low level of knowledge about an indicator or relationship. The analyst looks for what appears to be happening and uses those observations to establish a starting point for further investigation.'],
['Trend analysis','Trend analysis looks at data over time. A useful comparison requires comparable periods and clearly defined measures. The analyst can ask whether an indicator increased or decreased and how quickly the change occurred.'],
['Estimation','Estimation uses available information to project a value that is not directly available. The handbook describes combining information from different sources to estimate something that no single source contains by itself.'],
['Choose the strategy from the question','If the question asks what the distribution looks like, a visual display may be a useful start. If it asks how an indicator changed over time, trend analysis is relevant. If little is known about an indicator, exploration can establish a baseline. If the target value is unavailable, estimation may help.'],
['A chart does not prove causation','Suppose a line chart shows customer complaints falling after a new manager starts. The chart supports an observed change. It does not, by itself, prove the manager caused the reduction. Other changes may have occurred during the same period.'],
['Comparing periods carefully','Trend analysis becomes misleading when periods are not comparable. Comparing one month with twelve months, or comparing a holiday period with an ordinary month, can create a pattern that is really a difference in exposure or context.'],
['Estimate with assumptions visible','An estimate is not a hidden fact. State which evidence was used, what relationship supports the projection, and what assumptions or uncertainty remain. This makes the estimate useful without pretending it is directly observed.'],
['Mini-analysis challenge','Suppose weekly sales are 100, 110, 105, 130 and 150. Visualisation can reveal the overall shape. Exploration can identify the unusually high weeks. Trend analysis can compare movement across the period. But before recommending action, the analyst should ask what other evidence explains the changes.'],
['Study application','For one small dataset, name the question first, choose one strategy, explain why it fits, perform the basic analysis, write the observation, then write a separate interpretation. This separation is a core beginner discipline.']
],
5:[
['Purpose is the anchor','The handbook’s process begins with purpose and questions because analysis exists to support a real use. A purpose defines what the work is intended to accomplish. A good analyst keeps returning to that purpose when deciding what evidence, method and output are appropriate.'],
['Turn purpose into questions','“Understand customer experience” is broad. “Which service issues appear most often in customer comments during the last month?” is more actionable. A specific question gives the analyst something that available evidence can address.'],
['Data and methods follow the question','Once the questions are clear, identify relevant evidence and select procedures that fit the evidence. This is why method should not be chosen simply because it is popular or technically impressive.'],
['Interpretation adds meaning carefully','A finding describes a pattern in relation to the question. Interpretation considers what that finding may mean and what the evidence can support. Interpretation should not silently become a causal claim when the evidence only shows association or difference.'],
['Reporting connects the work','The handbook emphasises writing key findings and interpretations in relation to the purpose. A useful report should connect narrative, tables and figures rather than presenting disconnected numbers. Methodology should also be clear enough for the audience to understand how the result was produced.'],
['Evaluation is part of analysis','After reporting, review the process. Was the team appropriate? Was the plan followed? Was data stored well? Did the writing communicate the evidence effectively? Evaluation identifies what worked and what should improve in the next analysis.'],
['Linear and cyclical models','A linear model is useful for planning: purpose → questions → data → analysis → findings → reporting. Real analysis can be cyclical: a finding may create a new question, which may require additional evidence or a different procedure. The models are complementary rather than mutually exclusive.'],
['Capstone scenario','A school wants to understand why attendance changed. Purpose: support attendance planning. Questions: when did attendance change, for which groups, and what contextual information might help explain the change? The analyst can combine quantitative attendance records with qualitative explanations rather than jumping directly to a conclusion.'],
['Final readiness check','You should now be able to define data and information, distinguish quantitative and qualitative evidence, identify appropriate strategies, explain the role of purpose and questions, separate observation from interpretation, and describe why analysis may need to cycle back to earlier steps.']
]}};

